// firestore.rules
//
// Security rules for ClassMate's Firestore database.
//
// How to apply these:
//   Option A (Firebase Console): Firestore Database -> Rules tab ->
//     paste this file's contents -> Publish.
//   Option B (Firebase CLI): reference this file as "firestore.rules"
//     in firebase.json, then run `firebase deploy --only firestore:rules`.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Real multi-teacher collaboration (this phase)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Classrooms are now shared documents at classrooms/{classroomId} —
// any member listed in that document's `memberUids` array can read and
// write it (see services/memberService.js for the roles: owner,
// teacher, viewer). A teacher's list of accessible classrooms is a
// separate pointer collection, users/{uid}/classroomRefs, which only
// that uid can read or write.
//
// What this enforces:
//   - Only a member of a classroom (their uid is in its `memberUids`
//     array) can read or write it at all.
//   - Only the classroom's stated owner (ownerUid) can create it in the
//     first place, or delete it.
//   - A teacher can only read/write their own classroomRefs and users
//     documents (used for the one-time migration flag) — never anyone
//     else's.
//   - The old teachers/{uid}/classrooms/ location from before sharing
//     existed is kept read/delete-able by its original owner ONLY —
//     needed for the one-time migration in
//     services/workspaceService.js, which reads then deletes from
//     there. Nothing new is ever written to this location.
//
// KNOWN SIMPLIFICATION (see Phase 1 explanation in project history):
// `update` here does not yet distinguish "teacher" from "viewer" — any
// current member can write. A viewer being unable to write, and a
// teacher being unable to edit `members`/`ownerUid`/`memberUids`
// directly, are follow-up hardening for when the viewer role and
// invitations actually ship, not implemented yet.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// "Join a Classroom" (co-teacher self-service, via a shared code)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// PROPOSED — added alongside the client code that needs it
// (repositories/firestoreClassroomRepository.js's createJoinCodeMapping/
// getClassroomIdByJoinCode/addSelfAsTeacher), but not yet verified
// against a real Firestore project: this sandboxed environment has no
// real Firebase credentials, so every other rule change in this
// project's history could be tested end-to-end — this one could not.
// Please review and test this specifically before deploying, the same
// way you would for any other security-rule change.
//
// The problem this solves: a co-teacher isn't a member of the
// classroom they're trying to join yet, so the existing `allow read`
// rule above (member-only) can't help them find it, and a query across
// `classrooms` filtered by join code would be denied for the same
// reason. Two additions, kept as narrow as possible:
//
//   1. A new joinCodes/{code} collection — a tiny, low-sensitivity
//      lookup (`{ classroomId: "..." }`) that reveals only an opaque
//      classroom id for a known code, nothing about the classroom's
//      actual content (no student names, no scores, nothing). Readable
//      by any signed-in user; write-once (create, never update/delete)
//      so a code can't be silently repointed at a different classroom
//      after being shared.
//
//   2. classrooms/{classroomId}'s `allow update` rule gains a second
//      path: a non-member may write, but ONLY if the resulting document
//      still has every field identical to before except their own
//      uid being added to `memberUids` and `members` — they cannot
//      touch scores, students, settings, ownerUid, or remove/modify
//      any other member's entry. This is exactly why
//      addSelfAsTeacher() was written as a narrow, additive-only update
//      (arrayUnion + a single new map key) rather than a full document
//      overwrite: a rule permitting "add exactly your own uid" is
//      possible to write safely; a rule permitting "a non-member may
//      write anything" is not.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Student join codes (no student authentication, by design)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Students join with no sign-in, no PIN, no password (see this
// project's CHANGELOG for the architecture discussion) — the
// classroom's student-facing code is the only thing standing between
// "anyone" and the roster. That means, unlike joinCodes above:
//   - studentJoinCodes/{code} must be readable with NO auth at all
//     (request.auth is null for every student device).
//   - Once resolved, the classroom document itself must also be
//     readable with no auth — Firestore has no way to say "readable
//     only if you arrived via a valid code," so the code itself (an
//     unguessable 6-character string) is the actual gate, the same way
//     a classroom's own Firestore document ID being unguessable is
//     what makes this safe at all, not authentication.
//   - WRITES stay restricted to authenticated members/owner, with one
//     narrow exception below for marking a student as joined.

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /classrooms/{classroomId} {
      // Split into get/list rather than one blanket `read`, verified
      // against actual usage: every access anywhere in this app's
      // repository code is doc(db, 'classrooms', classroomId) — a
      // direct read by a known ID — never a collection query. So
      // `get` needs to support the no-auth student flow (see below),
      // but there is no legitimate reason for `list` to ever succeed
      // for anyone, member or not — nothing in the app queries this
      // collection broadly, and permitting it would let an
      // unauthenticated client enumerate/scan classroom documents
      // instead of only fetching one they already have the ID or
      // student code for.
      allow get: if (request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in resource.data.memberUids) || true;
      // The `|| true` above is deliberate, not a mistake — see the
      // "Student join codes" comment block below: gets must be open to
      // unauthenticated students once they have a classroomId, which
      // in practice only ever happens via an unguessable code. Left
      // as two conditions rather than a bare `allow get: if true` so
      // the intent (member OR anyone-with-the-id) stays legible.
      allow list: if false;

      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid == request.resource.data.ownerUid &&
        request.auth.uid in request.resource.data.memberUids;

      allow update: if request.auth != null && (
        request.auth.uid in resource.data.memberUids ||
        isSelfOnlyJoin(request, resource)
      );

      allow delete: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid == resource.data.ownerUid;
    }

    match /joinCodes/{code} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null;
      allow create: if request.auth != null;
      allow update, delete: if false;
    }

    match /studentJoinCodes/{code} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow create: if request.auth != null;
      allow update, delete: if false;
    }

    // NOT YET SUPPORTED: markStudentJoinedPortal() in workspaceService.js
    // needs an unauthenticated student device to flip one nested flag
    // (a student's hasJoinedPortal, buried inside teams[].students[])
    // — and there's no safe way to write a rule permitting "change
    // exactly this one deeply-nested field, nothing else" the way
    // isSelfOnlyJoin() does for the simpler top-level members/
    // memberUids case below. Writing a rule permissive enough to allow
    // this would also allow an unauthenticated visitor to rewrite
    // scores, students, or anything else in the document — not an
    // acceptable trade. Left deliberately unaddressed here rather than
    // either silently broken or unsafely open — see this file's
    // accompanying explanation for the recommended fix (restructuring
    // this flag to a top-level field, which a rule CAN safely permit).

    match /users/{uid} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid;

      match /classroomRefs/{classroomId} {
        allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid;
      }
    }

    // This rule's own create/update condition no longer
    // cross-references classrooms/{classroomId}/studentAuthLinks —
    // that collection, and the enrollment step that populated it,
    // were rolled back (see project history). A student write is
    // now authorized by a direct match between the document's own
    // uid field and the caller's real request.auth.uid instead — see
    // this rule's own comment below for the exact condition.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/studentGoals/{goalId} {
      // Readable by the goal's own linked student, or any teacher
      // member of this classroom.
      //
      // Checks resource.data.uid directly, NOT a studentAuthLinks
      // get() keyed by resource.data.studentId (an earlier version
      // of this rule did, and Firestore denied every list query
      // against this collection outright as a result — confirmed
      // directly: Firestore cannot prove a list operation satisfies a
      // rule that depends on another document's own fields, resolved
      // per potential result, and refuses the whole query rather than
      // risk it. uid is denormalized onto each document at write time
      // instead (see firestoreStudentGoalsRepository.js's own
      // submitGoal()), where a single-document get() against
      // studentAuthLinks is always safe — removing the dependency
      // from the read path entirely. The teacher-check get() below,
      // against classrooms/$(classroomId), is unaffected: classroomId
      // is this rule's own static path variable, not a per-document
      // field, so Firestore can prove it holds for every possible
      // result of the query.
      allow read: if request.auth != null && (
        request.auth.uid == resource.data.uid ||
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids
      );

      // A student may create ONLY their own goal — proven via a
      // direct match between the document's own uid field and the
      // caller's real, authenticated request.auth.uid (not a
      // client-supplied studentId taken at face value). Deliberately
      // does not cross-reference a studentAuthLinks mapping — that
      // collection, and the enrollment step that populated it, were
      // rolled back (see project history); this is the minimum,
      // direct check that keeps student goal writes working without
      // it. May only ever create with status 'pending_approval' —
      // never 'approved'. Deliberately NOT broadened for this
      // milestone's own completion-toggle feature — that write is an
      // update to an already-existing, already-approved goal, never
      // a create; see allow update below for its own, separate rule.
      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid &&
        request.resource.data.status == 'pending_approval';

      // Three, deliberately separate ways a studentGoals document may
      // be updated — kept as an explicit allow update (not folded
      // into allow create, update above) so each grant stays legible
      // on its own, per explicit product decision:
      allow update: if request.auth != null && (
        // 1) A student may keep editing their own goal's own text
        // while it's still pending — unchanged from this rule's own
        // original create/update condition.
        (
          request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid &&
          request.resource.data.status == 'pending_approval'
        ) ||
        // 2) A teacher member may approve — but ONLY flip status to
        // 'approved', touching no other field on this document.
        // Unchanged.
        (
          request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids &&
          request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['status']) &&
          request.resource.data.status == 'approved'
        ) ||
        // 3) The goal's own linked student may toggle daily
        // completion on their own, ALREADY-approved goal — proven
        // against resource.data.uid (the document's own, existing,
        // already-verified owner), never a client-supplied value;
        // request.resource.data.uid == resource.data.uid additionally
        // guarantees this same write can't smuggle in a uid change
        // alongside the completion fields. Scoped to exactly the five
        // completion-related fields via affectedKeys().hasOnly(...) —
        // status, text, uid, and every other field remain exactly as
        // untouchable here as they are in branch 1/2 above.
        (
          request.auth.uid == resource.data.uid &&
          request.resource.data.uid == resource.data.uid &&
          resource.data.status == 'approved' &&
          request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(
            ['completions', 'completedToday', 'currentStreak', 'longestStreak', 'overallCompletionPercent']
          )
        )
      );

      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    // Class Feed
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // Mirrors studentGoals' own, already-proven pattern exactly: a
    // student writes only as themselves (request.resource.data.uid ==
    // request.auth.uid), never a client-supplied studentId taken at
    // face value.
    //
    // HONEST NOTE, not a new weakening: `allow read` here is
    // deliberately broader than studentGoals' own read rule — any
    // classmate genuinely needs to see every post in a shared feed,
    // which studentGoals never required. This is real, and worth its
    // own explicit acknowledgment: without a studentAuthLinks-style
    // mapping (removed earlier in this project's history), no rule
    // anywhere in this file — including studentGoals' own existing
    // create rule — can cryptographically prove a given anonymous uid
    // genuinely belongs to a real student in THIS classroom, as
    // opposed to some other classroom's own anonymous device. Feed
    // posts inherit exactly the same trust level every other
    // student-authored collection in this file already has today;
    // this is not a new gap introduced for this feature.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/feedPosts/{postId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null;

      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid;

      // Any authenticated classmate may touch reactorUids/commentCount
      // on ANY post — reacting to or commenting on someone else's
      // post is the entire point, so this is deliberately not
      // author-restricted. Firestore rules can't cheaply verify a
      // student only adds/removes their own uid from the array
      // without significantly more complex logic; accepted as a
      // low-stakes, explicit MVP trade-off — the worst case is a
      // cosmetic miscount, not a privacy or security issue. A
      // teacher member may additionally flip removedByTeacher and/or
      // media, and ONLY those fields.
      allow update: if request.auth != null && (
        request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['reactorUids', 'commentCount']) ||
        (
          request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids &&
          request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['removedByTeacher', 'media'])
        )
      );

      allow delete: if request.auth != null &&
        (resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid ||
         request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids);

      match /comments/{commentId} {
        allow read: if request.auth != null;

        allow create: if request.auth != null &&
          request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid;

        allow delete: if request.auth != null &&
          (resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid ||
           request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids);

        allow update: if false;
      }
    }

    // Scoreboard Archive — one immutable snapshot per reset, created
    // only by a teacher (classroom member) performing the Reset
    // Scoreboard action (see services/scoreboardArchiveService.js).
    // Deliberately allows no update or delete at all, at the rules
    // level: "the archive must be read-only" and "never overwrite the
    // previous archive" are enforced here, not merely by the app
    // choosing not to expose an edit/delete control.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/scoreboardArchives/{archiveId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow update, delete: if false;
    }

    // Learning Programmes — ProgrammeSession history (see
    // models/ProgrammeSession.js, services/programmeSessionRepository.js).
    // Config-sized Learning Programme data itself
    // (classroom.learningProgrammes) lives on the classroom document
    // above and needs no rule of its own; this block covers only the
    // unbounded session-history subcollection.
    //
    // PROPOSED, NOT YET VERIFIED against a live Firebase project —
    // this sandbox has no Firebase CLI or live Firestore project to
    // test against, the same limitation already documented elsewhere
    // in this project's history for other rules additions. Please
    // review and test this specifically before deploying.
    //
    // Mirrors the existing classroom-membership convention exactly
    // (see the scoreboardArchives block below, and feedPosts above):
    // any current classroom member may read or create a session.
    // Update is also member-scoped, since a session is genuinely
    // recorded incrementally over the course of a real occurrence
    // (attendance, then goals, then observations) rather than written
    // once — unlike scoreboardArchives, which is a one-shot immutable
    // snapshot. Delete is disallowed at the rules level, matching this
    // project's own historical-integrity invariants for Learning
    // Programmes: a session should never simply disappear.
    //
    // NOTE — a pre-existing gap discovered during this work, NOT
    // something this change is responsible for and NOT modified here
    // per this phase's own "do not modify unrelated Firestore rules"
    // instruction: neither classrooms/{id}/lessons/{lessonId} (Planner)
    // nor classrooms/{id}/resources/{resourceId} (Learning Hub) has an
    // explicit rule anywhere in this file. Both currently fall through
    // to the deny-by-default catch-all below. Flagged here for the
    // reviewing team's own awareness; out of scope for this phase.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/programmeSessions/{sessionId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow update: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    // Learning Circle Student Data Boundary — Phase 1: membership
    // identity foundation ONLY. See the security audit and
    // architecture design this Phase was authorized from (project
    // history) for the full reasoning; summarized here.
    //
    // WHAT THIS ESTABLISHES: one device's own per-slot anonymous
    // Firebase Auth uid, self-attested as corresponding to a specific
    // studentId's ACTIVE membership in a specific Learning Programme,
    // verified against that programme's own real membership data at
    // the moment of creation — not merely trusted at face value the
    // way a bare client-supplied studentId would be.
    //
    // WHY THIS DOCUMENT'S OWN ID IS THE UID, NOT THE STUDENTID: so a
    // FUTURE rule (StudentEntry, not part of this Phase) can resolve
    // "does the caller's own uid correspond to an active member of
    // this programme" via a single, concrete get() keyed directly by
    // request.auth.uid — never a query, never a search across many
    // documents, and therefore never subject to the exact limitation
    // already documented on the studentGoals rule above (Firestore
    // cannot prove a rule holds for every possible result of a LIST
    // operation when that rule depends on another document's own
    // per-result-varying field; a single get() by a value fixed for
    // the whole request has no such problem).
    //
    // WHY THE CREATE RULE CAN VERIFY ACTIVE MEMBERSHIP WITHOUT A
    // SEPARATE learningProgrammes SUBCOLLECTION: LearningProgramme
    // documents don't exist as their own collection today — they're
    // embedded in classroom.learningProgrammes[], each with its own
    // memberships[] array. The nested .exists() macro below (a
    // documented Firestore Rules/CEL capability for searching a list
    // of maps by an arbitrary predicate, including a nested list
    // inside each map) can inspect that embedded structure directly,
    // without requiring it to first be promoted to a real
    // subcollection. This exact nested-array check was NOT verified
    // against a live or emulated Firestore project in this sandbox
    // (no network access, no Firestore CLI/emulator available here,
    // consistent with every other limitation already documented
    // throughout this project's history) — flagged explicitly as a
    // required verification step before this is considered
    // production-ready, not something to assume works purely because
    // it reads correctly.
    //
    // WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH: cryptographic proof of a real-
    // world student's identity. This app's own Student Portal
    // onboarding (see ui/student-portal/onboarding/StudentDeviceFlow.js)
    // is, by explicit product decision, a classroom code plus
    // self-service roster-name selection — no verified enrollment
    // step exists anywhere in this app. This document inherits that
    // exact trust ceiling; it does not exceed it. What it DOES
    // establish is real and specific: this uid has self-attested a
    // studentId that, at creation time, genuinely had an active
    // membership record in this specific programme — closing the
    // cross-student data-boundary gap this Phase exists to fix,
    // without claiming to solve identity verification more broadly.
    //
    // studentId/status can never be changed after creation (`allow
    // update: if false`) — a new device always produces a brand-new
    // link document (a different uid), never a rewrite of an old one.
    // Nobody deletes these (`allow delete: if false`), matching every
    // other immutable-record convention already used throughout this
    // file (ProgrammeSession, StudentGoals' own append-only fields).
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/learningProgrammes/{programmeId}/membershipLinks/{uid} {
      // Readable by the link's own uid, or any teacher member of this
      // classroom — mirrors studentGoals' own read rule shape exactly
      // (a direct match against the caller's own identity, OR
      // existing teacher/classroom-owner authorization via
      // memberUids), so teacher access here is exactly as compatible
      // with existing authorization as every other classroom-scoped
      // collection already is.
      allow read: if request.auth != null && (
        uid == request.auth.uid ||
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids
      );

      // A student may create ONLY their own link (the document ID
      // must equal their own real, authenticated uid — never a
      // client-supplied value for someone else), containing ONLY the
      // three documented fields, with status fixed to 'active' at
      // creation (a link is never created in any other state), AND
      // only when the claimed studentId genuinely has an active
      // membership record in THIS specific programme right now — the
      // nested .exists() check below reads that directly from the
      // classroom document's own, already-real membership data,
      // never trusting the claim on its own.
      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        uid == request.auth.uid &&
        request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['studentId', 'joinedAt', 'status']) &&
        request.resource.data.studentId is string &&
        request.resource.data.status == 'active' &&
        get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.learningProgrammes.exists(p,
          p.id == programmeId &&
          p.memberships.exists(m, m.studentId == request.resource.data.studentId && m.status == 'active')
        );

      allow update: if false;
      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    // PHASE 3 — Student Identity & Learning Circle Data Boundary.
    // See the architecture design and this Phase's own authorization
    // (project history) for the full reasoning; summarized here.
    //
    // WHY THIS EXISTS: ProgrammeSession mixes every roster student's
    // own attendance, goals, and observations into one document.
    // Firestore cannot expose part of a document to a client — a
    // student can never safely be granted `allow read` on the
    // canonical session document above without leaking every other
    // student's own data. This is the minimum, independently-
    // addressable slice that CAN be safely, narrowly exposed.
    //
    // WHY TWO get() HOPS ARE NEEDED TO RESOLVE THE STUDENT'S OWN
    // MEMBERSHIP LINK: `studentEntries` lives under
    // `programmeSessions/{sessionId}`, which has no `programmeId` in
    // its own PATH (only as a FIELD on the session document) — so a
    // rule here first reads that one session document (safe: keyed
    // by `sessionId`, a static path variable for this whole match,
    // never a per-result-varying value) to learn its own
    // `programmeId`, THEN reads that programme's own
    // `membershipLinks/{request.auth.uid}` (also safe: a single
    // document keyed directly by the caller's own known, fixed uid —
    // never a search, never a list). Both of these are exactly the
    // kind of get() this rules file has already established are safe
    // for a single-document evaluation (see membershipLinks' and
    // programmeSessions' own rule comments); neither is a list
    // operation, so neither runs into the "cannot prove for every
    // possible result" limitation documented elsewhere in this file.
    //
    // WHY ONE EXCEPTION TO THIS FILE'S "NO CUSTOM FUNCTIONS" STYLE:
    // every other rule in this file inlines its own get() calls
    // directly. Here, the SAME two get() calls would otherwise need
    // to be repeated up to four times across the read/create/update
    // branches below — a real efficiency and readability cost, not
    // just a stylistic one. Firestore Rules' own `function` + `let`
    // support exists specifically for this: compute an intermediate
    // value once, reuse it. Scoped narrowly to this one match block,
    // not a rewrite of this file's own broader convention.
    //
    // WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH: cryptographic proof of a real-
    // world student's identity — this inherits the exact same trust
    // ceiling membershipLinks itself already does, no more, no less
    // (see that rule's own comment above for the full reasoning).
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/programmeSessions/{sessionId}/studentEntries/{studentId} {
      function isTeacherMember() {
        return request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;
      }

      // Resolves this session's own programmeId (one get(), reused
      // below), then that programme's own membershipLinks entry for
      // THIS caller's own uid (a second get(), also reused) — true
      // only if that link's own claimed studentId matches the path
      // segment being read/written AND that membership is currently
      // active.
      function isLinkedActiveStudent() {
        let sessionProgrammeId = get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)/programmeSessions/$(sessionId)).data.programmeId;
        let link = get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)/learningProgrammes/$(sessionProgrammeId)/membershipLinks/$(request.auth.uid));
        return link.data.studentId == studentId && link.data.status == 'active';
      }

      // PHASE 3.2 — closes the exact gap Phase 3.1's own review found:
      // the top-level hasOnly(['goals']) check below says nothing
      // about what's INSIDE goals.<categoryId>, so a direct,
      // non-UI write could otherwise set outcome/reflection — fields
      // the product intends as teacher-only — on a student's own
      // write. These two functions are what actually close it.
      //
      // WHY THE SHAPE CHECK ALLOWS ALL FOUR KEYS, NOT JUST
      // text/source: traced directly against
      // models/ProgrammeSession.js's own createProgrammeGoalEntry() —
      // a BRAND NEW goal, created by recordGoal() (called identically
      // whether the caller is a student or a teacher picking a new
      // suggestion), ALWAYS produces all four keys explicitly —
      // `outcome: null, reflection: ''` are real, explicit defaults
      // on every legitimate write, never omitted. A rule requiring
      // hasOnly(['text', 'source']) alone would reject the student's
      // own ordinary, correct goal-setting flow. The actual boundary
      // that matters is not WHICH KEYS exist, but WHAT VALUES
      // outcome/reflection are allowed to hold in a student-authored
      // write: only their own untouched defaults, never anything
      // else — which is exactly what a teacher's own
      // recordGoalOutcome() call is the one and only path that ever
      // changes.
      //
      // WHY create AND update NEED SEPARATE, DIFFERENTLY-SHAPED
      // CHECKS: for a CREATE, `resource` doesn't exist yet, so there
      // is nothing to diff against — every category in the freshly-
      // written goals map is, by definition, part of THIS write. For
      // an UPDATE, checking the WHOLE resulting goals map would be
      // wrong — a student editing category B must not be blocked
      // because a DIFFERENT category (A) already has a real,
      // teacher-set outcome from before; only the categories THIS
      // specific write actually changes should ever be checked. A
      // NESTED diff (on the `goals` map itself, not the whole
      // document) isolates exactly that. `resource.data.get('goals',
      // {})` — not direct dot access — specifically so a student
      // setting their OWN first-ever goal, after a teacher has
      // already created this document with only `attendance` (no
      // `goals` key at all yet), doesn't throw trying to diff against
      // a field that doesn't exist yet.
      function isValidStudentGoalCreate() {
        return request.resource.data.goals.keys().all(categoryId,
          request.resource.data.goals[categoryId].keys().hasOnly(['text', 'source', 'outcome', 'reflection']) &&
          request.resource.data.goals[categoryId].outcome == null &&
          request.resource.data.goals[categoryId].reflection == ''
        );
      }

      function isValidStudentGoalUpdate() {
        let priorGoals = resource.data.get('goals', {});
        let changedCategoryIds = request.resource.data.goals.diff(priorGoals).affectedKeys();
        return changedCategoryIds.all(categoryId,
          request.resource.data.goals[categoryId].keys().hasOnly(['text', 'source', 'outcome', 'reflection']) &&
          request.resource.data.goals[categoryId].outcome == null &&
          request.resource.data.goals[categoryId].reflection == ''
        );
      }

      // Either a teacher (classroom member) or this exact, linked,
      // currently-active student — never any other student, never an
      // unauthenticated caller, and never a read of the PARENT
      // programmeSessions document itself (that remains governed
      // exclusively by its own rule above, unchanged).
      allow read: if request.auth != null && (isTeacherMember() || isLinkedActiveStudent());

      // CREATE — the document doesn't exist yet, so there is no
      // `resource` to diff against; check the proposed document's own
      // top-level keys directly instead. A teacher may create with
      // `attendance` and/or `goals` in one write — exactly the same
      // fields they're already allowed to touch on UPDATE below, so
      // CREATE isn't more restrictive than UPDATE for no real reason.
      // A student may create with ONLY `goals` — their own first
      // goal for this session — never `attendance`, matching this
      // Phase's own explicit "student cannot modify attendance"
      // requirement — AND only with the field-ownership shape
      // isValidStudentGoalCreate() above enforces.
      allow create: if request.auth != null && (
        (isTeacherMember() && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['attendance', 'goals'])) ||
        (isLinkedActiveStudent() && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['goals']) && isValidStudentGoalCreate())
      );

      // UPDATE — the document already exists; scope each side to only
      // the field(s) they're ever allowed to touch, using a real diff
      // against the real, existing resource, so an existing field the
      // OTHER party already wrote (e.g. a student's own `goals` next
      // to a teacher-written `attendance`) is never required to be
      // re-submitted or accidentally clobbered. The student's own
      // branch is additionally scoped by isValidStudentGoalUpdate()
      // above — the teacher's own branch is intentionally untouched
      // by any of this, preserving every existing teacher privilege
      // exactly as it was.
      allow update: if request.auth != null && (
        (isTeacherMember() && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['attendance', 'goals'])) ||
        (isLinkedActiveStudent() && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['goals']) && isValidStudentGoalUpdate())
      );

      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // Legacy, pre-sharing location — migration-only (see
    // services/workspaceService.js). Nothing new is ever written here.
    //
    // MIGRATION NOTE for this new Firebase project specifically: this
    // rule exists to support a one-time read-then-delete migration
    // from data that predates classroom sharing. A brand-new Firestore
    // database has no documents at this path at all — there is
    // nothing to migrate from here. Harmless to keep (an unreachable
    // rule for a collection with no data isn't a security risk), but
    // it is genuinely dead weight in this project specifically. Safe
    // to delete this block entirely if you'd rather keep the rules
    // file lean; keeping it only makes sense if you plan to import
    // data from the old project into this same legacy path.
    match /teachers/{teacherId}/classrooms/{classroomId} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == teacherId;
    }

    // Everything else is denied by default (Firestore rules are
    // deny-by-default outside of explicit `match` blocks), but this is
    // here for clarity and as a deliberate backstop.
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}

// A non-member may update a classroom ONLY to add exactly their own
// uid to memberUids/members — nothing else may change. Every other
// top-level field (teams, notebooks, settings, learningActivities,
// ownerUid, classroomJoinCode, etc.) must be byte-for-byte identical
// to what it was before the write.
function isSelfOnlyJoin(request, resource) {
  return request.auth.uid in request.resource.data.memberUids &&
    !(request.auth.uid in resource.data.memberUids) &&
    request.resource.data.memberUids.size() == resource.data.memberUids.size() + 1 &&
    request.resource.data.members.diff(resource.data.members).affectedKeys().hasOnly([request.auth.uid]) &&
    request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['members', 'memberUids']);
}
